A||||14||GT Bastard||Wheel & tire combo vs. TRACTION and acceleration...||||1||
Z||000000||GT Bastard||12-18-2000||10:30 PM||||Here's the deal... I want a wheel and tire combo that is LIGHT(for good acceleration), gives GOOD TRACTION, and looks nice.  Above all, I want traction and acceleration.  My current combo is stock 14's with a set of sticky Yokohama's(1FST2M6's choice.)  It handles well and sticks good on hard launches, but I'm not a big fan of stock rims, and I'd like to have some extra traction for the new motor.  I also want lower profile tires for auto-x. My question is this:   I realize that a taller wheel and tire combo will effectively increase my gear ratio(slower acceleration), but will it allow for better traction on launches?<P>I'm looking at the 15 inch Konsei's and Yokohama AVS Intermediate's(205/50/15's in front and 215/50/15's in back.)  They look like a kick-a$$ value.  Any thoughts?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Xtreme Fiero's||12-18-2000||10:45 PM||||You will proably get the best performance form a set of 14" wheels and some 215/60 Yoko A032's the lower the profile of the tire the sooner it will "break loose" on the track 1/4 mile or Auto-X||||reg||1
Z||000002||1FST2M6||12-19-2000||08:29 AM||||Kosei K1s are KILLER!!! AVS are too! you'll be JUST fine.. the reason i had to ditch the 17s was the rotating mass.. the K1s your looking at only tip the scales at 12lbs!!! less than the stock 14s! jump all over it!! as for the A032s they only size you they make that'll even be close on our cars is a 225-50-15 (WAY wrong size) and a 185-60-14 ususlly run on triumphs/CRX/other tiny cars in SCCA IT racing. they are Super sticky I have a pair on a set of panasport S/Ls I was on the 185s when Scott (SCCA PRO IT driver) pulled my car to 1.13g at Road Atlanta in July of 99. (GT Bastards car)<P><BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sig1fst.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-19-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Philphine||12-19-2000||11:27 AM||||the last sport compact car mag. i got (i don't know if another has come out yet so it may be the most recent) had as it's cover story a big article on wheel/tire sizing and it's affect on acceleration and handling. pretty interesting.||||reg||1
Z||000004||hugh||12-19-2000||09:21 PM||||You may get slightly better traction,because of the larger diameter your tire will have a little longer footprint.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Hawk||12-20-2000||11:25 AM||||You will love the Kosei K-1 wheels and AVS tires, I had that combination on a miata I had, that sucker cornered like it was on rails, I think I paid $110 per wheel for 15X7 rims. Great value for a light wheel!<P>Chris saul<BR>87 GT, 5 speed<BR>soon to be 3.4l||||reg||1
Z||000006||JC Webb||12-21-2000||09:45 AM||||1FST2M6 wrote:  As for the A032s they only size you they make that'll even be close on our cars is a 225-50-15 (WAY wrong size)<P>I have 225-50-15 Dunlop SP 8000 on my 86 SE 2M6 and absolutely love them.  What is wrong with this size?||||reg||1
Z||000007||1FST2M6||12-21-2000||10:43 AM||||they are to short... the stock tire is a 215-60-15 (GT) you installed a tire that is 1.31" shorter... your Speedo will read 60 when your only running 52. there is nothing wrong with it just reads the speedo wrong and leaves a larger gap in the wheel well. the stock tire is 25.16" tall the 225-50 is 23.8" tall.  On the SEs i've seen with this set up they look 4wd even lowered. I'm running 235-40 with are .7 shorter then the stock tire. which makes my speedo read 60 when i'm runnin 58.  the smaller tire is better if your screaming past a cop at least you think you are.. and his raydar says your ok. just asteticly a bit different and can cuase additional bearing wear and higher rpms on the highway and subsequintly lower gas mileage.<P>SP 8000s are killer tires! don't get me wrong! just the size is kinda screwey. casue our cars fall on their face now above 60ish.. and with the higer RPMs needed to keep the shorter tires turning to keep up with the competition the motors run out of breath. the taller tires aid a bit in our need for long-legedness. thats what Ive found anyway. the 205-60s i had on my SE were great for 1/8mi or stop light bashing but in the 1/4 or on the highway the motor couldn't do enough to make it really competative. just with the math the 225s will be even more hard pressed to hang with much on the highway. <p>[This message has been edited by 1FST2M6 (edited 12-21-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||12-21-2000||12:32 PM||||so....if you're thinking of keeping you're over all diameter close to stock, do you figure from the 15" wheel on gt's, or the 14's that the rest had (not really considering the 13" base wheel, never had any)? i've never had a gt and for my 16x7.5's i've been considering 225/45-16's to put me back to about right. i may stay with 50 series cause they're easier to find and get deals on.||||reg||1
Z||000009||1FST2M6||12-21-2000||12:56 PM||||use what ever it came with as a guide...  use this formula  215*.60/25.4*2+14<P>section width X aspect ratio X 2(top and botom of tire) + wheel diameter=total height<P>with a 245-45-16  24.6" tall  a 255-45 would be closer at 25.03"||||reg||1
Z||000010||terryk||12-21-2000||02:21 PM||||The smaller the tire, the more torque applied to the ground.||||reg||1
Z||000011||1FST2M6||12-21-2000||02:47 PM||||kinda||||reg||1
Z||000012||JC Webb||12-21-2000||06:37 PM||||Thanks 1FST2M6, you have a good point.  While stoplight to stoplight the smaller tires may have an advantage, taller ones are better for cruising the interstate.  I am ready to replace my rear tires.  The SP 8000's on the rear lasted 16 months and 5,000 miles thanks to a miss-aligned rear suspension.  I plan on replacing the rears with cheaper  245-60-15 Dunlop GT Qualifiers.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Will||12-22-2000||09:50 AM||||I have Dunlop GT Qualifiers in that size on the rear (235/60's in front).<BR>You're right, they're cheap. You'll be taking a performance hit by making that switch, though.<BR>On the upside, I've seen almost 30 mpg on the highway (2.8 w/ Getrag), the small wheel house gaps look good, and the increased ground clearance means I don't have to be wary of speed bumps as I would if the car were lowered.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||80's BOY||12-23-2000||09:53 AM||||i have Bridgestone 195/55/16's (i think) Potenza series tires.||||reg||2
